{"name":"State construction apprentice-to-journeyman ratio requirements by trade","description":"Apprentice-to-journeyman ratio requirements for construction trades in each US state. Each record is one state and one trade or program, giving the initial ratio (how many journeymen for the first apprentice), the ratio thereafter (how many additional journeymen per additional apprentice), and whether it applies to public works, private, or both. Answers 'what is the apprentice-to-journeyman ratio for [trade] in [state]' and 'how many journeymen do I need before I can hire another apprentice on this job'. Ratios vary widely, and states structure them differently: some (MN) delegate to individual JATC programs, each with its own ratio; others (CA) set a state-wide hourly ratio on public works with trade-specific exemptions.","url":"https://referencesource.org/state-apprenticeship-ratios-by-trade/","licence":"Facts from state labor department pages and apprenticeship regulations; ratio requirements are not copyrightable","last_verified":"2026-09-03","stale_after":"2027-09-03","sources":["https://www.dir.ca.gov/das/RatioExemption.htm","https://www.dli.mn.gov/business/workforce/apprenticeship-ratios"],"records":[{"state":"California","trade_or_program":"California Apprenticeship Council (default)","initial_ratio":"one apprentice hour for every five journeyman hours","thereafter_ratio":"one apprentice hour for every five journeyman hours","scope":"public works","computation_basis":"hourly","statute_citation":"Labor Code 1777.5 (g)","id":"california-apprenticeship-council-default-california","url":"https://referencesource.org/state-apprenticeship-ratios-by-trade/california-apprenticeship-council-default-california/","source":"https://www.dir.ca.gov/das/RatioExemption.htm","source_quote":"If you agree to be governed by the regulations set forth by the California Apprenticeship Council, then the minimum and maximum ratio is the same: a total of one apprentice hour for every five journeyman hours for the project as a whole.","verified_fields":["initial_ratio","state","statute_citation","thereafter_ratio"],"derived_fields":["computation_basis","scope","trade_or_program"],"located_fields":["initial_ratio","thereafter_ratio"]},{"state":"California","trade_or_program":"Laborers – Northern California","initial_ratio":"not required to hire an apprentice until there are four journeymen on the jobsite","scope":"public works","statute_citation":"Labor Code 1777.5 (g)","id":"laborers-northern-california-california","url":"https://referencesource.org/state-apprenticeship-ratios-by-trade/laborers-northern-california-california/","source":"https://www.dir.ca.gov/das/RatioExemption.htm","source_quote":"Participating contractors are not required to hire an apprentice until there are four journeymen on the jobsite","verified_fields":["initial_ratio","statute_citation"],"derived_fields":["scope","trade_or_program"],"located_fields":["initial_ratio"]},{"state":"California","trade_or_program":"Laborers – Southern California","initial_ratio":"not required to hire an apprentice until there are four journeymen on the jobsite","scope":"public works","statute_citation":"Labor Code 1777.5 (g)","id":"laborers-southern-california-california","url":"https://referencesource.org/state-apprenticeship-ratios-by-trade/laborers-southern-california-california/","source":"https://www.dir.ca.gov/das/RatioExemption.htm","source_quote":"Approved contractors are not required to hire an apprentice until there are four journeymen on the jobsite","verified_fields":["initial_ratio","statute_citation"],"derived_fields":["scope","trade_or_program"],"located_fields":["initial_ratio"]},{"state":"California","trade_or_program":"Operating Engineers – Northern California","initial_ratio":"not less than one apprentice for each four journeymen","thereafter_ratio":"not less than one apprentice for each four journeymen","scope":"public works","statute_citation":"Labor Code 1777.5 (g)","id":"ope